From 200786064c6295bda1aaf3504c590ba289227906 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: laidick <laidick@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 14:46:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] dsv41: a prompt cuts in by the tokens it computes, and with
 pictures its kept state holds

TF_FILL_CUT_IN compares a queued prompt's whole length (LaneHost.admitOne, Engine.addStream), so a turn of a long
conversation never cuts in between another pass's pieces: 300K tokens cached and 1.5K new is a 301.5K-token prompt,
although its pass computes 1.5K from the kept state it resumes. That is most requests of an agent workload, and on a
pair one waited 33.7 s behind a cold 360K prompt with 1.6K tokens of its own to compute. A prompt with pictures
never cuts in either (engine.cutsIn, Lanes.prefillPart), even when every picture is wholly before its kept state
and the pass runs no tower and sends no rows (0043's pictureNeedsRows).

The test now takes the tokens the prompt computes: its length less the kept state it resumes. LaneHost.admitOne asks
the cache where a queued request would resume (Store.find, which changes nothing; a state only on disk counts as
none, so a prompt waiting for a restore does not cut in), and Engine.addStream reads the stream's reuse.at. A
prompt cuts in with pictures when none ends past that point (Engine.picturesPast, the same test as
pictureNeedsRows). Unchanged: one cut-in at a time, nothing cuts into a pass with pictures, and a prompt that needs
picture rows waits. If the kept state is gone by the time its pass opens, the cut-in runs from 0 whole, as a long
prompt that waited would have, and the paused pass resumes after it.
